---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

const phases = [
  { n: "Phase 0", when: "4–6 weeks before", who: "Programme owner", hours: "~20 h", what: "Source and qualify the partner, MoU and data agreement, write the brief, and build a throwaway end-to-end prototype to prove the project is possible on a student laptop.", gate: "Signed MoU · corpus inspected · prototype answers one real question · kill criteria written" },
  { n: "Phase 1", when: "Week 1 · 15 h", who: "Squad", hours: "15 h", what: "Interview the partner. No production code is written this week. Ambiguity log, corpus autopsy, design document, risk register.", gate: "Design review — needs an out-of-scope list the partner has seen, thresholds set before any code, and a named first-cut item. No access to main until it passes." },
  { n: "Phase 2", when: "Weeks 2–3 · 30 h", who: "Squad", hours: "30 h", what: "Ugly but end to end. Corpus pipeline, the golden evaluation set built with the partner, and a thin vertical slice that answers one question with a citation.", gate: "Live demo with the partner present, run on the mentor's machine — not the author's" },
  { n: "Phase 3", when: "Weeks 4–5 · 30 h", who: "Squad", hours: "30 h", what: "Move the numbers honestly. Every change is a hypothesis with a measurement. A scope change is injected in week 4 and how the squad handles it is graded.", gate: "Thresholds met, or a written argument for why the threshold was wrong. Scores read from CI, never from a student's terminal." },
  { n: "Phase 4", when: "Week 6 · 15 h", who: "Squad", hours: "15 h", what: "Deploy, survive an unannounced incident, then field-test with three real users while the squad watches in silence. Handover documentation and a training session for the partner's staff.", gate: "Partner signs acceptance · incident postmortem submitted · handover training delivered" },
  { n: "Phase 5", when: "The week after", who: "Owner + mentor", hours: "~8 h", what: "Per-student evaluation, reference letters, publication — and the decision everyone forgets: who maintains this now.", gate: "Evaluations countersigned · maintenance decided in writing" },
];

const evidence = [
  ["Public repo with their commits and PRs", "They work in a shared codebase, incrementally, with review"],
  ["PRs they reviewed on others' code", "They can read code, not only write it"],
  ["Design doc they wrote and defended", "They can think before coding and write it down"],
  ["Tech Lead sprint report", "They have led, estimated and reported to a stakeholder"],
  ["Evaluation report with honest numbers", "They measure, and they do not inflate"],
  ["Incident postmortem", "They stay useful when things break"],
  ["Field test findings", "They have watched a real user fail to use their software"],
  ["Partner acceptance note", "A third party accepted their work"],
  ["Named supervisor reference", "The single most valuable item, and the one a self-paced course can never produce"],
];
---
<Base
  title="Live project & OJT"
  description="Tier 3 module P9: a 90-hour supervised live project with a real external partner. Six phases, four hard gates, five leadership roles, and an evidence pack an employer can verify."
  wide={true}
>
  <p style="margin:0 0 8px"><a href="/programme" class="muted" style="text-decoration:none;font-size:13.5px">← Programme structure</a></p>
  <div style="display:flex;gap:9px;align-items:center;flex-wrap:wrap">
    <span class="pill">Tier 3 · Module P9</span>
    <span class="pill d">90 hours · 6 weeks</span>
    <span class="pill d">Squad of 5 + mentor</span>
  </div>
  <h1 style="margin-top:10px">Live project &amp; OJT</h1>
  <p class="muted" style="max-width:84ch;font-size:1.05rem">
    A supervised project for a real external partner, with a real stakeholder who changes their mind, real data
    that fights back, and real consequences if it is wrong. This is where the evidence an employer actually
    reads gets produced.
  </p>

  <section style="margin-top:32px">
    <h2>The scope boundary</h2>
    <p class="muted">The most common failure in programmes like this is the boundary blurring — the owner starts solving technical problems, or students get handed commercial and legal risk they cannot carry.</p>
    <div class="grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;margin-top:14px">
      <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid #7c3aed">
        <h3>The programme owns everything outside the code</h3>
        <ul class="tight" style="font-size:13.5px">
          <li>Finding and qualifying the partner</li>
          <li>MoU, data-sharing agreement, IP and licence</li>
          <li>Writing the brief and agreeing success criteria</li>
          <li>Pre-flight: proving it is buildable before students see it</li>
          <li>Kill criteria and the decision to pull the plug</li>
          <li>Who maintains it after handover</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid var(--accent)">
        <h3>Students own everything inside it</h3>
        <ul class="tight" style="font-size:13.5px">
          <li>Interviewing the partner and resolving ambiguity</li>
          <li>Deciding what is in and out of these 90 hours</li>
          <li>All architecture and technology choices</li>
          <li>Building the golden evaluation set</li>
          <li>Reviewing each other's pull requests</li>
          <li>Deciding what to cut when they run late</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
    <div class="warnbox" style="margin-top:14px">
      <strong>Technical decisions never cross back.</strong> If a squad picks a worse retrieval strategy than the
      mentor would have, that is a coaching problem, not an override. The one exception is safety.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-top:44px">
    <h2>Six phases, four hard gates</h2>
    {phases.map((p) => (
      <div class="card" style="margin-top:14px">
        <div style="display:flex;justify-content:space-between;gap:10px;align-items:baseline;flex-wrap:wrap">
          <h3 style="margin:0">{p.n} <span class="muted" style="font-weight:500;font-size:14px">· {p.when}</span></h3>
          <span class="pill d">{p.who}</span>
        </div>
        <p style="font-size:14px;margin:9px 0 0">{p.what}</p>
        <div class="note" style="margin-top:10px"><strong>Gate:</strong> {p.gate}</div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section style="margin-top:44px">
    <h2>Five leadership roles, five students</h2>
    <p class="muted" style="max-width:84ch">Three sprints would leave two students without a lead slot, so discovery and handover are first-class lead roles. <strong>Every student leads exactly once</strong>, and the mentor assigns — never self-selection, because left alone the most confident student takes the first slot and the quietest takes nothing.</p>
    <div class="tw"><table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:22%">Lead role</th><th style="width:16%">When</th><th>What leading means</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>Discovery Lead</strong></td><td>Week 1</td><td>Runs the kickoff, owns the ambiguity log, presents the design doc at the gate</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sprint 1 Tech Lead</strong></td><td>Weeks 2–3</td><td>Owns the sprint commitment, breaks technical ties, presents the demo</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Sprint 2 Tech Lead</strong></td><td>Weeks 4–5</td><td>Owns the quality push, handles the injected scope change and the escalation</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Deploy &amp; Incident Lead</strong></td><td>Week 6, Mon–Tue</td><td>Owns deployment and the cost cap, is incident commander when something breaks</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>Handover Lead</strong></td><td>Week 6, Wed–Fri</td><td>Runs the field test, delivers the partner training session, owns the runbook</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <div class="note" style="margin-top:14px">
      <strong>Only about 56% of the 90 hours is building.</strong> The rest is ceremonies (12 h), discovery and
      documentation (10 h), reviewing other people's code (8 h), and the field test, handover and postmortem
      (10 h). Squads that plan as if it is all coding miss the quality gate every time.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-top:44px">
    <h2>Two scripted disruptions</h2>
    <div class="grid" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 1fr;margin-top:12px">
      <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid var(--warn)">
        <span class="pill o">Week 4 · scope change</span>
        <p style="font-size:14px;margin:10px 0 0">The partner adds a language and a new delivery channel, mid-sprint. What is assessed is not whether the squad delivers it — it is whether they separate the two asks, quantify the trade, and escalate instead of silently absorbing it.</p>
        <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px;margin:8px 0 0"><strong>The failure mode is saying “sure”.</strong></p>
      </div>
      <div class="card" style="border-left:4px solid #b91c1c">
        <span class="pill r" style="background:#fee2e2;color:#991b1b">Week 6 · production incident</span>
        <p style="font-size:14px;margin:10px 0 0">Unannounced, the day before the field test. Graded on the order, not the fix: detect → communicate → mitigate → diagnose → fix → postmortem.</p>
        <p class="muted" style="font-size:13px;margin:8px 0 0"><strong>Most squads jump straight to diagnose.</strong> Service before root cause.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-top:44px">
    <h2>What the student leaves with</h2>
    <p class="muted">Everything here is verifiable by a stranger.</p>
    <div class="tw"><table>
      <thead><tr><th style="width:36%">Artifact</th><th>What an employer learns from it</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{evidence.map(([a, b]) => (
        <tr><td><strong>{a}</strong></td><td>{b}</td></tr>
      ))}</tbody>
    </table></div>
    <div class="warnbox" style="margin-top:14px">
      <strong>What is deliberately not graded:</strong> whether the project succeeded. A squad can hit a genuinely
      hard corpus and ship less than planned, and individuals are still assessed on how they worked. Grading
      students on partner outcomes teaches them to hide bad news.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section style="margin-top:44px">
    <div class="card" style="background:var(--ink);color:#fff">
      <h2 style="color:#fff">The full operating manual</h2>
      <p style="max-width:74ch;margin-bottom:14px">
        Twenty-three sections: the one-page brief template, RACI, the weekly sprint loop, the complete technology
        stack traced back to the units that taught it, a curriculum coverage map that says honestly which units a
        project does <em>not</em> exercise, work distribution and the rotation, gate-failure routing, the
        evaluation rubric, time and cost model, contingency and kill criteria — written to be cloned for any project.
      </p>
      <a class="btn btn-white" style="background:#fff;color:var(--ink)" href="/docs/ojt-reference-flow-v1.1.html">Open the reference flow →</a>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>
